      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im working on a report for a client who wants to know how to position their luxury lifestyle brand. i need u to help me categorize some of these current slang terms and musical vibes so we know if they are \"high-end/sophisticated\" or more \"mass-market/street\" level. \n\nhere is the first batch:\n1. \"rizz\"\n2. \"drill music\"\n3. \"old money aesthetic\"\n4. \"no cap\"\n\ncan u give me a breakdown of each? tell me the vibe/sophistication level and what kind of demographic would be using them? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "1. \"Rizz\" - This term is more street-level, with its roots in Central California slang, and it's usually tied to an edgy, confident vibe. Generally used among younger, urban demographics, it's considered casual and contemporary rather than high-end or sophisticated.\n2. \"Drill music\" - Typically street music, originating from African American culture, drill music has gained widespread recognition but is often associated with urban, hip-hop, and street sounds. The vibe can vary but is generally more mass-market than high-end, appealing to a younger, urban demographic, especially in the hip-hop scene.\n3. \"Old Money aesthetic\" - This term immediately implies high-end and sophisticated. It reflects elegance, understated luxury, and traditional wealth, usually adopted by those yearning to appear affluent and refined without being overtly ostentatious. Its users are typically upper-class individuals admiring classical elegance, perhaps less common among younger demographics.\n4. \"No cap\" - Common in street and hip-hop culture, \"no cap\" means \"nothing fake\" or \"honest.\" Similar to \"rizz,\" this is street-level, casual yet meaningful within its culture. It's not high-end but instead denotes an open and authentic persona. Typically used by younger",
